WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.160
Model storage should balance control with startup performance.

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:09.300
A managed repository can hold approved model versions, checksums, licenses, and configuration metadata.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:17.280
Workers can copy approved artifacts into local SSD caches so inference does not depend on sustained access to shared storage.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:25.020
Define cache size, eviction policy, and how a worker verifies that its local copy matches the version advertised to the router.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.560
Use explicit version identifiers rather than a mutable label such as latest.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:35.780
Model weights, quantization, tokenizer, prompt template, runtime, and serving parameters together influence behavior.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.200
Record that combination so results can be reproduced and a problematic release can be rolled back.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:49.340
Stage model changes on limited capacity, run representative evaluations, and promote only after functional and performance checks pass.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.680
Separate model artifacts from private operational data.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:01:02.420
Prompts, outputs, uploaded files, embeddings, vector indexes, and logs can contain sensitive information even when the model weights are public.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:09.280
Apply access controls, retention schedules, encryption, and backup policies according to data classification.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:15.100
Avoid placing private data in a broadly readable model share simply because every worker can access it.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:26.120
Back up what cannot be recreated: configuration, routing policy, secrets references, evaluation results, metadata, and authorized private datasets.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:32.020
Large public model files may be restored from trusted sources if their exact versions and hashes are recorded.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.800
Test restoration rather than equating a completed backup job with recoverability.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:44.700
Storage design succeeds when a new or recovered node can obtain the right model safely, start predictably, and avoid exposing unrelated data.
